In France, voting is traditionally a low-tech experience: voters feel isolated and alone, selecting their chosen candidate by folding a pre-printed paper ballot into an envelope.
After officials confirmed the voter's identity, the voter drops the ballot into the ballot box and signs the ballot paper.
French election law strictly codifies these procedures to a fairly wide extent.
Since 1988, election ballots must be transparent in order for voters and observers to be able to provide testimony that ballot boxes were absent at the beginning and that no additional ballot boxes were added beyond those properly counted and authorized voters.
Candidates may send their representatives to be present at all stages of the process. During evening hours, votes are counted by volunteers under strict supervision and in accordance with special guidelines.
